  "title": "Credential Exposure",
  "content": "Credential exposure occurs when passwords, API keys, session tokens, recovery codes, or account lists become available to someone who should not have them. Exposure can happen through public repositories, configuration backups, shared documents, screenshots, logs, phishing, or reused credentials from another breach. A secret should be treated as compromised once an unauthorized party could have copied it; deleting the visible page does not make the secret trustworthy again.\n\n<b>Immediate response</b>\nPreserve the minimum evidence needed to identify the source, then revoke or rotate affected secrets. For user accounts, force secure password resets, invalidate active sessions and recovery tokens, and verify multifactor authentication settings. For service credentials, replace the secret everywhere it is used and restart or redeploy dependent services safely. Review authentication and access logs for suspicious use before and after the known exposure window. Prioritize privileged, shared, and externally accessible accounts.\n\n<b>Remove the source, not only the symptom</b>\nClean the public file, repository history, artifact store, cache, or deployment package that caused the leak. Search for duplicates and related secrets. Notify the system owner and incident response team through approved channels; notification requirements may depend on the data, jurisdiction, and organization.\n\nPrevention combines a password manager, unique credentials, phishing-resistant multifactor authentication where available, least privilege, automated secret scanning, short-lived tokens, and deployment rules that keep backups and environment files out of public paths. Never paste real secrets into tickets, chat, or reports. Refer to an identifier and use an approved secure channel for replacement material.",
  "links": [
    "leaked credentials",
    "public code repository",
    "sensitive credentials"
  ]
}
